/* generated by tools/video_shotlist_science.py from optima-4th-science/data/videos: rerun after adding a lesson */
window.SHOTLIST={"lessons":[{"w":10,"d":"01","title":"Lesson 10.01: Web of Life","file":"lesson-10-01-web-of-life.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-10-01-web-of-life.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · open the case (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-welcome","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Hi, scientists! Welcome back to the Field Station. Today’s case starts with a tree.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Before the oak · Before the owl]\n\nThis giant live oak stood in a Florida hammock, a shady patch of forest. A barred owl lived up in its limbs.\n\n[ON SCREEN: After the fallen oak · After the mushrooms]\n\nThen a storm knocked it over. Look what showed up on the trunk. Hmm. Only ONE tree fell, but the owl left, the squirrels left, and mushrooms moved in.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat’s our case: why did so much change?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nEvery game you finish adds a clue to “My clues.” Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · tie the threads (Clue 1) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-warmup","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Let’s meet the oak’s neighbors.\n\n(Hold up a piece of string.) Scientists draw a line, or a thread, for every connection between living things.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The five threads]\n\nHere are five kinds of threads. Only one is about eating!\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to tie]\n\nTap a visitor to read what it does with the oak. Then drag the thread that fits, or tap the thread and then the visitor. One visitor isn’t even alive. Can you spot it?\n\nWhen all five are tied, list what you found in the boxes. Press Listen. Dr. Reyes has a tip for you.","want":true},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · pull a thread (Clue 3) (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-part3","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Hold a string web or a cat’s cradle, then tug one string.) Watch. I pull one string, and look how many others move!\n\n[ON SCREEN: The web]\n\nThis is the web around Dr. Reyes’s oak. Each colored line is one connection.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Predict first]\n\nPick a thread to pull. Before you pull, predict: tap every living thing you think will change. Then press Pull the thread and see how close you were. Try all three.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie. One of these pulls surprised Dr. Reyes!","want":true},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · the saw palmetto (Clue 4) (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-explore","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Saw palmetto]\n\nHave you seen this plant? Saw palmetto grows all over Florida. Its stems have tiny teeth, like a saw. Ouch!\n\nIt’s short, but it has a web of its own.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Five boxes]\n\nRead about it, then drag each card into its kind of connection. One card goes in each box. That’s your last clue!\n\nPress Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie first.","want":true},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-assignment","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! At the top of this tab, crack the case: why did so much change when only one tree fell?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen write your case report: two short answers, your own ecosystem web, and a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Help buttons]\n\nWatch the chips light up as you write. Stuck? Press Hint. Your answers save as you type.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-evidence","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nSome people say, “As long as we have plants and animals, the small stuff doesn’t matter.” Bees. Mushrooms. Bacteria. Hmm. Think about the threads you pulled.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER: your claim, your evidence from today, and your reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap your side, then two pieces of evidence. They drop right into your boxes. The reasoning is all you. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants to hear your side.","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-closing","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Case closed! One tree was the center of a whole web.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and a live oak acorn goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time, we’ll look at real numbers from Florida. How can you tell if a place is healthy, or out of balance?\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.","want":true},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · the real live oak (Clue 2) (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-part1","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The live oak photo]\n\nThis is a real live oak, the same kind as Dr. Reyes’s tree. See those little ferns on the limbs? Open every number, then match each kind of connection to a number. That’s Clue 2! Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":false},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check (15–20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Quick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.","want":false},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · your own ecosystem web (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-partc","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Your place]\n\nNow it’s your web. Pick a Florida place you know. Name five living things there, then three connections, each a different kind. Want to draw it? The sketch pad is a bonus.","want":false},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-vocab","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-lesson","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · four kinds of connections (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-part2","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B (30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-partb","done":false,"script":""}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"oak","label":"📷 Before the oak"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"owl","label":"📷 Before the owl"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"fallen","label":"📷 After the fallen oak"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"fungus","label":"📷 After the mushrooms"},{"label":"🗂 The case question","tag":"CASE FILE 10","title":"The Day the Big Oak Fell","col":"#B9822A","body":"Why did so much change when only one tree fell?"},{"label":"🧭 Your clue bar","tag":"HOW TO SOLVE IT","title":"Find 4 clues","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🗂 My clues","The bar under the tabs. Tap it to see your clues."],["? ? ? ?","Each game you finish fills in a clue."],["🔒","Crack the case at the top of the Assignment."]]}],"warmup":[{"label":"🧵 The five threads","tag":"WARM-UP GAME","title":"Five kinds of threads","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🍽️","Food"],["🌼","Pollination"],["🏠","Shelter"],["♻️","Nutrients"],["🤲","Human care"]]},{"label":"👆 How to tie","tag":"HOW TO PLAY","title":"Drag or tap","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["1","Tap a visitor to read what it does."],["2","Drag a thread onto it, or tap the thread, then the visitor."],["3","Tie all five, then list what you found."]]}],"part3":[{"label":"🕸️ The web","tag":"THE WEB AROUND THE OAK","title":"Each line is one connection","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🌳","Live oak in the middle"],["🐿️ 🦉 🐝","Tied to the oak"],["🌴 🐻","Two threads away"]]},{"label":"🔮 Predict first","tag":"HOW TO PLAY","title":"Guess, then pull","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","Pick a thread to pull."],["2","Tap the living things you think will change."],["3","Press Pull the thread and check your guess."]]}],"explore":[{"label":"🌴 Saw palmetto","tag":"PART 4","title":"A short palm with fan-shaped fronds","col":"#2F7A55","body":"It grows all over Florida, often right under big oaks."},{"label":"🗃 Five boxes","tag":"THE SORT","title":"One card in each box","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🍽️","Food"],["🌼","Pollination"],["🏠","Shelter"],["♻️","Nutrients"],["🤲","Human care"]]}],"assignment":[{"label":"🔓 Crack the case","tag":"TOP OF THE ASSIGNMENT","title":"Why did so much change?","col":"#B9822A","body":"Pick the answer that fits all four clues."},{"label":"✍️ Your case report","tag":"WHAT TO WRITE","title":"Four places to write","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Two short answers"],["Part C","Your own ecosystem web"],["CER","Claim, evidence, reasoning"],["📋","Copy your Gathered Answers into Canvas"]]},{"label":"🛟 Help buttons","tag":"STUCK?","title":"Help under every box","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["Your answer needs","Chips light up as you write."],["Hint","Three hints, one at a time."],["📎 My evidence","Your highlights and photo evidence."]]}],"evidence":[{"label":"💬 The quote","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"Some people say…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“As long as we have plants and animals, we don’t need to worry about the small stuff like bees, fungi, and bacteria.”"},{"label":"⚖ CER","tag":"REMEMBER CER?","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["C","Claim: what you think is true."],["E","Evidence: facts from the lesson that prove it."],["R","Reasoning: how the facts prove your claim."]]},{"label":"🧩 The planner","tag":"PLAN BEFORE YOU WRITE","title":"Tap to plan","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","Tap I agree or I disagree."],["2","Tap two pieces of evidence."],["3","They drop into your C and E boxes. You write the R."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"🌰 Your specimen","tag":"FIELD NOTEBOOK","title":"Live oak acorn","col":"#B9822A","body":"Finish the checklist to stamp CASE CLOSED."},{"label":"➡ Next time","tag":"LESSON 10.02","title":"Healthy or out of balance?","col":"#2F7A55","body":"Real Florida numbers."}],"part1":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"liveoak","label":"📸 The live oak photo"}],"quiz":[],"partc":[{"label":"🗺️ Your place","tag":"PART C","title":"Pick a Florida place","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🏖️","A beach"],["🏡","A backyard"],["🐸","A pond"],["🌲","A pine forest"]]}]}},"words":[{"w":"Ecosystem","d":"All the living things and nonliving things — like soil, water, and air — in one place, working together.","e":"🌳"},{"w":"Habitat","d":"The place where a living thing finds food, water, shelter, and space. The oak tree is a habitat for many small animals.","e":"🏡"},{"w":"Pollination","d":"When pollen moves from one flower to another so seeds can form. Bees, butterflies, and even wind can pollinate plants.","e":"🐝"},{"w":"Pollinator","d":"An animal that carries pollen from flower to flower. Bees, butterflies, hummingbirds, and even bats can be pollinators.","e":"🦋"},{"w":"Nutrients","d":"The materials in soil and food that plants and animals need to grow and stay healthy.","e":"🟤"},{"w":"Web of life","d":"All the connections — eating, pollinating, sheltering, breaking down — that tie living things together in an ecosystem.","e":"🕸️"}]},{"w":12,"d":"01","title":"Lesson 12.01: Sensing the World","file":"lesson-12-01-sensing-the-world.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-12-01-sensing-the-world.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · open the case (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-welcome","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Hi, scientists! Welcome to the Field Station. Today you're solving a real mystery.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach · CAM 2 woods · CAM 3 river · CAM 4 marsh]\n\nThese pictures came from four trail cameras in Florida: a baby sea turtle, a panther, a manatee and a cottonmouth snake.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The times]\n\nLook at the clocks. All four animals moved within about two minutes, and the cameras were far apart. Hmm. I wonder why.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat's our case: what made them all react, and how did each one know?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nSee \"My clues\" under the tabs? Every game you finish turns a question mark into a clue. Four clues cracks the case.\n\nType your first name on the ID card. Then press Listen on Dr. Reyes's walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · the snap test (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-warmup","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Let's start with you, because your body does this too.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The snap test]\n\nUnder the walkie-talkie is a game called \"Hear the snap.\" Tap the picture, then wait. Don't guess! When you hear the snap, tap fast. Try it three times. Watch me.\n\n(Do the test once on camera. Read your time out loud.)\n\nI didn't stop and think. My body just did it!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your steps]\n\nThen write what happened in your body, in order, from the sound to your tap. There are no wrong answers yet.\n\nHow did your body decide that fast? Press Listen and hear what Dr. Reyes thinks.","want":true},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall · act it out (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-vocab","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Four of today's words are steps, so let's act them out. Stand up if you can!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Stimulus]\n\nStimulus: something happens. (Snap your fingers.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Sense organ]\n\nSense organ: a body part notices it. (Point to your ear.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Brain]\n\nBrain: it decides. (Tap your head.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Response]\n\nResponse: you do something. (Turn around.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Act it out]\n\nFaster: snap, ear, head, turn! That's your snap test.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Word Lens]\n\nNow tap each word sticker below me. Dr. Reyes reads the word and what it means. Get a green check on all six.","want":true},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson · what’s on this tab (25–30 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-lesson","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: This tab]\n\nThis tab has four parts and three clues. Part 1 teaches the four steps, then you play Send the Signal for clue two. Part 2 is about different senses, with an alligator picture. In Part 3 you open Dr. Reyes's four cameras for clue three. And in Part 4, one more camera shows a heron. Sort its steps for clue four. Go in order, and watch your clue bar.","want":false},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · the four steps + Send the Signal (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-part1","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Let's name what happened in your snap test.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The four steps]\n\nEvery reaction has four steps, in order. A stimulus: the snap. A sense organ notices it: your ears. Your brain decides: \"Something's behind me!\" And the response: you tapped. All four can happen in less than one second!\n\nThink about your warm-up list. Did you write a step for your brain? Lots of people skip it, because it's so fast.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Send the signal]\n\nBelow the reading is Send the Signal. Drag each step onto a station, starting out in the world. Get all four right and watch the signal run. That's clue two!\n\nFirst, press Listen. Dr. Reyes has something to say about the middle steps.","want":true},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · different senses (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-part2","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"An eagle spots a fish with its eyes. A panther hears a rustle with its ears.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Five senses]\n\nEach sense organ picks up something different. Eyes pick up light, ears pick up sound, your nose picks up smell, your tongue picks up taste, and your skin picks up touch, heat and cold.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Watch out]\n\nWatch out for one mistake: the brain can't notice anything by itself. No sense organ, no signal. No signal, no response. Remember that for the end of the assignment.","want":false},{"id":"figure","label":"Teacher · The alligator picture (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-figure","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Alligator pathway]\n\nRead this picture left to right, like a sentence.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Read it left to right]\n\nA splash: that's the stimulus. The alligator's ear: the sense organ. Its brain decides, \"Food!\" Then it turns toward the splash: the response. The yellow lines show the message traveling. Can you say all four steps without looking?","want":true},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · open the four cameras (Clue 3) (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-part3","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Time to look at the evidence, like a real field scientist.\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to open a camera]\n\nBelow the walkie-talkie are four trail cameras. Tap one, then press Next step to follow the message through that animal, one step at a time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach]\n\nFor each one, ask: what started this reaction? Was it the same thing for all four? That's clue three.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The manatee photo]\n\nThen tap the numbers on the manatee photo to see how it feels the water.\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes will tell you what to watch for.","want":true},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · the heron on CAM 5 (Clue 4) (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-explore","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The heron]\n\nCAM 5 caught a great blue heron hunting, one minute after the other cameras.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The heron story]\n\nRead the story slowly. The heron stands still. A fish swims by. The heron freezes, then strikes. I notice it froze first. I wonder why?\n\n[ON SCREEN: One card, sorted]\n\nDrag each card into its step. Let's do one together: \"A small fish swims into view.\" That happens out in the water, before the heron does anything. So it's the stimulus! You sort the rest.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The hidden step]\n\nThe pink box is the brain. No card goes there, because no camera can see inside the heron's head. Remember that for your CER.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.","want":true},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check · how it works (20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Self-Check]\n\nThis Self-Check isn't graded. It's just for you. Answer one question, then press Next question. Picked a wrong answer? Read the hint. It tells you what to think about. Think about the four steps, and try again.","want":false},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case + roadmap (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-assignment","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"You made it to the assignment!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nFirst, crack the case. Still locked? Tap \"Show me where the clues are,\" then \"Go there.\" With all four clues, pick the answer that fits every clue. Did all four animals react to the same thing?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Today’s parts]\n\nThen there are three parts. Part B: two answers in complete sentences. Part C: make up your own Florida animal moment. A sketch is a bonus. And last, a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Saved for you]\n\nEverything saves itself and shows up in Gathered Answers at the bottom. That's what you turn in.\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants a real field report.","want":true},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B · short answers (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-partb","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Part B tools]\n\nAnswer three questions in complete sentences, using our step words. Under each box, the \"Your answer needs\" chips turn green as you include each part. Stuck? Press \"Need a hint?\" for one hint at a time. And \"My evidence\" adds anything you highlighted.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach · CAM 2 woods]\n\nFor question five, pick an animal from last night's cameras. They're right above the question.","want":false},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · your own animal + sketch (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-partc","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Now you're the scientist.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your scenario]\n\nMake up a short moment, like the heron story, about a Florida animal that wasn't in today's lesson. A pelican? A bobcat? A dolphin? Fill in the four boxes: what happened, what noticed it, what the brain decided, and what the animal did.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The sketch pad]\n\nIf you have time, sketch it too. It's a bonus. Draw with the pen. To add a label, pick Label, drag from a part of your drawing to an empty spot, and type. Add arrows to show the order.","want":true},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-evidence","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The classmate says]\n\nA classmate says, \"Animals just react. They don't really need a brain. Their senses do all the work.\" Hmm. Think about the hidden pink box from the heron. Do you agree?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER: your claim, your evidence from today's lesson, and your reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap \"I agree\" or \"I disagree,\" then tap two pieces of evidence. They drop right into your boxes! The reasoning is all you. Try starting with \"This shows that…\"","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed · finish strong (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-closing","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Case closed · Confetti]\n\nCase closed! You did it, scientists.\n\nFour animals noticed four different things with four different senses, and each one's brain decided what to do. The four steps solved it.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Before you go]\n\nTwo last things. Check every box in What to Turn In to earn a heron feather. And download your field journal.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes has a sneak peek at next time.","want":true}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam1","label":"📷 CAM 1 beach"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam2","label":"📷 CAM 2 woods"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam3","label":"📷 CAM 3 river"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam4","label":"📷 CAM 4 marsh"},{"label":"⏱ The times","tag":"NOTICE","title":"Look at the clock on each camera","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["9:14:08","CAM 1 · beach"],["9:14:31","CAM 2 · woods"],["9:15:02","CAM 3 · river"],["9:15:47","CAM 4 · marsh"]]},{"label":"🗂 The case question","tag":"CASE FILE 12","title":"The Night Everything Moved","col":"#B9822A","body":"What made four animals react at almost the same time, and how did each one know?"},{"label":"🧭 Your clue bar","tag":"HOW TO SOLVE IT","title":"Find 4 clues","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🗂 My clues","The bar under the tabs. Tap it to see your clues."],["? ? ? ?","Each game you finish fills in a clue."],["🔒","Crack the case at the top of the Assignment."]]}],"warmup":[{"label":"⏱ The snap test","tag":"WARM-UP · TRY IT FOR REAL","title":"Hear the snap","col":"#2F6FD6","rows":[["1","Tap the trail picture to start."],["2","Wait. Don’t guess!"],["3","The moment you hear SNAP, tap fast."],["3×","Try it three times."]]},{"label":"📝 Your steps","tag":"THEN WRITE","title":"What happened in your body?","col":"#2F6FD6","body":"Write at least three steps, in order. Start with the sound. End with you turning around."}],"vocab":[{"label":"🎭 Act it out","tag":"WORDS YOU’LL USE TODAY","title":"Act out the four steps","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["Stimulus","Snap your fingers."],["Sense organ","Point to your ear."],["Brain","Tap your head."],["Response","Turn around."]]},{"label":"🔭 Word Lens","tag":"HOW TO USE IT","title":"Tap a word sticker","col":"#7A5BB5","body":"Dr. Reyes reads the word and what it means. The words light up as she reads. Try to tap all six."}],"lesson":[{"label":"🗺 This tab","tag":"THE LESSON","title":"Four parts, three clues","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part 1","The four steps, and the Send the Signal game (Clue 2)"],["Part 2","Different senses and the alligator picture"],["Part 3","Open last night’s four cameras (Clue 3)"],["Part 4","One more camera: sort the heron’s steps (Clue 4)"]]}],"part1":[{"label":"🔁 The four steps","tag":"THE FOUR-STEP PATHWAY","title":"Every animal reaction","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["⚡ Stimulus","Something happens in the world: a sound, a smell, a flash of light."],["👂 Sense organ","A body part picks it up: eyes, ears, nose, tongue or skin."],["🧠 Brain","The brain decides what it means."],["🏃 Response","The animal does something."]]},{"label":"📡 Send the signal","tag":"THE GAME BELOW","title":"Put the steps in order","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Drag","Drag each step onto a station, or tap a step, then a station."],["Start","Start where the message starts: out in the world."],["Watch","Fill all four and watch the signal run. That’s Clue 2!"]]}],"part2":[{"label":"👀 Five senses","tag":"PART 2","title":"Each sense organ picks up something different","col":"#16807B","rows":[["Eyes","light"],["Ears","sound and vibration (tiny, fast shaking)"],["Nose","smell"],["Tongue","taste"],["Skin","touch, heat and cold, and pain"]]},{"label":"⚠ Watch out","tag":"A COMMON MISTAKE","title":"The brain can’t do it alone","col":"#C8553D","body":"No sense organ, no signal. No signal, no response."}],"figure":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"pathway","label":"🐊 Alligator pathway"},{"label":"➡ Read it left to right","tag":"READ THE PICTURE","title":"Left to right","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Splash","the stimulus"],["Ear","the sense organ"],["Brain","decides"],["Turn","the response"]]}],"part3":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam1","label":"📷 CAM 1 beach"},{"label":"📹 How to open a camera","tag":"LAST NIGHT’S TRAIL CAMERAS","title":"Open all four","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["Tap","Tap a camera to open it."],["Next step","Press Next step to follow the message one step at a time."],["Ask","What started this reaction?"]]},{"kind":"photo","pic":"manatee","label":"🦭 The manatee photo"}],"explore":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"heron","label":"🪶 The heron"},{"label":"📖 The heron story","tag":"CAM 5 · 9:16 PM","title":"A heron hunting","col":"#B9822A","body":"A great blue heron stands still in shallow water. A small fish swims into view. The heron freezes, then strikes. Its sharp beak grabs the fish."},{"label":"🃏 One card, sorted","tag":"WATCH ME SORT ONE","title":"A small fish swims into view.","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["Stimulus","It happens out in the water, before the heron does anything. It starts everything."]]},{"label":"🧠 The hidden step","tag":"THE PINK BOX","title":"No camera can see it","col":"#C8553D","body":"The brain step happens inside the heron. Keep it in mind: it’s great evidence for your CER."}],"quiz":[{"label":"🧠 Self-Check","tag":"NOT GRADED","title":"Just for you","col":"#2F6FD6","rows":[["One at a time","Answer, then press Next question."],["Oops?","Read the hint. It tells you what to think about."],["Score","See your score at the end."]]}],"assignment":[{"label":"🔎 Crack the case","tag":"CRACK THE CASE","title":"What happened that night?","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["🔒","Locked until you have all 4 clues."],["🔎","Missing one? Tap “Show me where the clues are.”"],["✔","Pick the answer that fits all four clues."]]},{"label":"📋 Today’s parts","tag":"YOUR ASSIGNMENT","title":"Three parts","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Answer 2 questions in complete sentences."],["Part C","Make up a Florida animal moment. A sketch is a bonus."],["CER","Agree or disagree with a classmate, using evidence."]]},{"label":"💾 Saved for you","tag":"TURNING IT IN","title":"Everything is gathered at the bottom","col":"#2F6FD6","body":"Every answer you type saves itself and shows up in Gathered Answers at the bottom of this tab."}],"partb":[{"label":"✍ Part B tools","tag":"PART B","title":"Help is built in","col":"#2F6FD6","rows":[["Your answer needs","The chips light up green as you include each part."],["💡 Need a hint?","One hint at a time."],["📎 My evidence","Use your highlights or photos as evidence."]]},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam1","label":"📷 CAM 1 beach"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam2","label":"📷 CAM 2 woods"}],"partc":[{"label":"🐾 Your scenario","tag":"PART C","title":"A made-up moment, like the heron story","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["Animal","A Florida animal NOT in today’s lesson."],["4 boxes","Stimulus, sense organ, brain, response."],["Ideas","Pelican, bobcat, anole lizard, armadillo, dolphin…"]]},{"label":"✏ The sketch pad","tag":"BONUS · SKETCH IT","title":"Draw, then label","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["✏️ Pen","Draw your animal and what it noticed."],["🏷️ Label","Drag from a part of your drawing to an empty spot, then type."],["Arrows","Show the order of the four steps."]]}],"evidence":[{"label":"💬 The classmate says","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"A classmate says…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“Animals just react. They don’t really need a brain. Their senses do all the work.”"},{"label":"⚖ CER","tag":"REMEMBER CER?","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["C","Claim: what you think is true."],["E","Evidence: facts from the lesson that prove it."],["R","Reasoning: how the facts prove your claim."]]},{"label":"🧩 The planner","tag":"PLAN BEFORE YOU WRITE","title":"Tap to plan","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","Tap I agree or I disagree."],["2","Tap two pieces of evidence."],["3","They drop into your C and E boxes. You write the R."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"🎉 Case closed","tag":"CASE CLOSED","title":"Four animals, four pathways","col":"#2F7A55","body":"Each animal noticed its own stimulus with its own sense organ, and its own brain decided what to do."},{"label":"✅ Before you go","tag":"FINISH STRONG","title":"Two last things","col":"#2F6FD6","rows":[["☑","Check off the boxes in What to Turn In."],["📓","Download your field journal, with your photos and sketch."]]},{"label":"🔜 Next time","tag":"NEXT LESSON","title":"Two animals, side by side","col":"#2F6FD6","body":"Build models of two Florida animals’ pathways and compare them."}]}},"words":[{"w":"Stimulus","d":"Something in the world that an animal can notice — a sound, a smell, a flash of light, a touch.","e":"⚡"},{"w":"Sense organ","d":"A body part that picks up information from the world. Eyes, ears, noses, tongues, and skin are all sense organs.","e":"👂"},{"w":"Brain","d":"The part of an animal that takes in information from the senses, decides what it means, and tells the body what to do.","e":"🧠"},{"w":"Response","d":"What an animal does after its brain decides — running, freezing, jumping, eating, hiding, calling out.","e":"🏃"},{"w":"Signal","d":"A tiny message that travels from a sense organ to the brain. Signals carry the information your senses pick up.","e":"📡"},{"w":"Information","d":"Something an animal learns from the world — like the smell of an animal that might eat it, the sight of food, or the sound of a splash.","e":"📨"}]}],"poems":[],"teacher":[],"teacherDocs":{"10-01":"# Lesson 10.01 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · open the case · g4sci-10-1-welcome\n\nHi, scientists! Welcome back to the Field Station. Today’s case starts with a tree.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Before the oak · Before the owl]\n\nThis giant live oak stood in a Florida hammock, a shady patch of forest. A barred owl lived up in its limbs.\n\n[ON SCREEN: After the fallen oak · After the mushrooms]\n\nThen a storm knocked it over. Look what showed up on the trunk. Hmm. Only ONE tree fell, but the owl left, the squirrels left, and mushrooms moved in.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat’s our case: why did so much change?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nEvery game you finish adds a clue to “My clues.” Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · tie the threads (Clue 1) · g4sci-10-1-warmup\n\nLet’s meet the oak’s neighbors.\n\n(Hold up a piece of string.) Scientists draw a line, or a thread, for every connection between living things.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The five threads]\n\nHere are five kinds of threads. Only one is about eating!\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to tie]\n\nTap a visitor to read what it does with the oak. Then drag the thread that fits, or tap the thread and then the visitor. One visitor isn’t even alive. Can you spot it?\n\nWhen all five are tied, list what you found in the boxes. Press Listen. Dr. Reyes has a tip for you.\n\n## Part 3 · pull a thread (Clue 3) · g4sci-10-1-part3\n\n(Hold a string web or a cat’s cradle, then tug one string.) Watch. I pull one string, and look how many others move!\n\n[ON SCREEN: The web]\n\nThis is the web around Dr. Reyes’s oak. Each colored line is one connection.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Predict first]\n\nPick a thread to pull. Before you pull, predict: tap every living thing you think will change. Then press Pull the thread and see how close you were. Try all three.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie. One of these pulls surprised Dr. Reyes!\n\n## Part 4 · the saw palmetto (Clue 4) · g4sci-10-1-explore\n\n[ON SCREEN: Saw palmetto]\n\nHave you seen this plant? Saw palmetto grows all over Florida. Its stems have tiny teeth, like a saw. Ouch!\n\nIt’s short, but it has a web of its own.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Five boxes]\n\nRead about it, then drag each card into its kind of connection. One card goes in each box. That’s your last clue!\n\nPress Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie first.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case · g4sci-10-1-assignment\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! At the top of this tab, crack the case: why did so much change when only one tree fell?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen write your case report: two short answers, your own ecosystem web, and a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Help buttons]\n\nWatch the chips light up as you write. Stuck? Press Hint. Your answers save as you type.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-10-1-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nSome people say, “As long as we have plants and animals, the small stuff doesn’t matter.” Bees. Mushrooms. Bacteria. Hmm. Think about the threads you pulled.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER: your claim, your evidence from today, and your reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap your side, then two pieces of evidence. They drop right into your boxes. The reasoning is all you. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants to hear your side.\n\n## Case closed · g4sci-10-1-closing\n\nCase closed! One tree was the center of a whole web.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and a live oak acorn goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time, we’ll look at real numbers from Florida. How can you tell if a place is healthy, or out of balance?\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.\n\n## Part 1 · the real live oak (Clue 2) · g4sci-10-1-part1\n\n[ON SCREEN: The live oak photo]\n\nThis is a real live oak, the same kind as Dr. Reyes’s tree. See those little ferns on the limbs? Open every number, then match each kind of connection to a number. That’s Clue 2! Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Self-Check · g4sci-10-1-quiz\n\nQuick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.\n\n## Part C · your own ecosystem web · g4sci-10-1-partc\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your place]\n\nNow it’s your web. Pick a Florida place you know. Name five living things there, then three connections, each a different kind. Want to draw it? The sketch pad is a bonus.\n","12-01":"# Lesson 12.01 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · open the case · g4sci-12-1-welcome\n\nHi, scientists! Welcome to the Field Station. Today you're solving a real mystery.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach · CAM 2 woods · CAM 3 river · CAM 4 marsh]\n\nThese pictures came from four trail cameras in Florida: a baby sea turtle, a panther, a manatee and a cottonmouth snake.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The times]\n\nLook at the clocks. All four animals moved within about two minutes, and the cameras were far apart. Hmm. I wonder why.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat's our case: what made them all react, and how did each one know?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nSee \"My clues\" under the tabs? Every game you finish turns a question mark into a clue. Four clues cracks the case.\n\nType your first name on the ID card. Then press Listen on Dr. Reyes's walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · the snap test · g4sci-12-1-warmup\n\nLet's start with you, because your body does this too.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The snap test]\n\nUnder the walkie-talkie is a game called \"Hear the snap.\" Tap the picture, then wait. Don't guess! When you hear the snap, tap fast. Try it three times. Watch me.\n\n(Do the test once on camera. Read your time out loud.)\n\nI didn't stop and think. My body just did it!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your steps]\n\nThen write what happened in your body, in order, from the sound to your tap. There are no wrong answers yet.\n\nHow did your body decide that fast? Press Listen and hear what Dr. Reyes thinks.\n\n## Word Wall · act it out · g4sci-12-1-vocab\n\nFour of today's words are steps, so let's act them out. Stand up if you can!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Stimulus]\n\nStimulus: something happens. (Snap your fingers.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Sense organ]\n\nSense organ: a body part notices it. (Point to your ear.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Brain]\n\nBrain: it decides. (Tap your head.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Response]\n\nResponse: you do something. (Turn around.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Act it out]\n\nFaster: snap, ear, head, turn! That's your snap test.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Word Lens]\n\nNow tap each word sticker below me. Dr. Reyes reads the word and what it means. Get a green check on all six.\n\n## The Lesson · what’s on this tab · g4sci-12-1-lesson\n\n[ON SCREEN: This tab]\n\nThis tab has four parts and three clues. Part 1 teaches the four steps, then you play Send the Signal for clue two. Part 2 is about different senses, with an alligator picture. In Part 3 you open Dr. Reyes's four cameras for clue three. And in Part 4, one more camera shows a heron. Sort its steps for clue four. Go in order, and watch your clue bar.\n\n## Part 1 · the four steps + Send the Signal · g4sci-12-1-part1\n\nLet's name what happened in your snap test.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The four steps]\n\nEvery reaction has four steps, in order. A stimulus: the snap. A sense organ notices it: your ears. Your brain decides: \"Something's behind me!\" And the response: you tapped. All four can happen in less than one second!\n\nThink about your warm-up list. Did you write a step for your brain? Lots of people skip it, because it's so fast.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Send the signal]\n\nBelow the reading is Send the Signal. Drag each step onto a station, starting out in the world. Get all four right and watch the signal run. That's clue two!\n\nFirst, press Listen. Dr. Reyes has something to say about the middle steps.\n\n## Part 2 · different senses · g4sci-12-1-part2\n\nAn eagle spots a fish with its eyes. A panther hears a rustle with its ears.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Five senses]\n\nEach sense organ picks up something different. Eyes pick up light, ears pick up sound, your nose picks up smell, your tongue picks up taste, and your skin picks up touch, heat and cold.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Watch out]\n\nWatch out for one mistake: the brain can't notice anything by itself. No sense organ, no signal. No signal, no response. Remember that for the end of the assignment.\n\n## The alligator picture · g4sci-12-1-figure\n\n[ON SCREEN: Alligator pathway]\n\nRead this picture left to right, like a sentence.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Read it left to right]\n\nA splash: that's the stimulus. The alligator's ear: the sense organ. Its brain decides, \"Food!\" Then it turns toward the splash: the response. The yellow lines show the message traveling. Can you say all four steps without looking?\n\n## Part 3 · open the four cameras (Clue 3) · g4sci-12-1-part3\n\nTime to look at the evidence, like a real field scientist.\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to open a camera]\n\nBelow the walkie-talkie are four trail cameras. Tap one, then press Next step to follow the message through that animal, one step at a time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach]\n\nFor each one, ask: what started this reaction? Was it the same thing for all four? That's clue three.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The manatee photo]\n\nThen tap the numbers on the manatee photo to see how it feels the water.\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes will tell you what to watch for.\n\n## Part 4 · the heron on CAM 5 (Clue 4) · g4sci-12-1-explore\n\n[ON SCREEN: The heron]\n\nCAM 5 caught a great blue heron hunting, one minute after the other cameras.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The heron story]\n\nRead the story slowly. The heron stands still. A fish swims by. The heron freezes, then strikes. I notice it froze first. I wonder why?\n\n[ON SCREEN: One card, sorted]\n\nDrag each card into its step. Let's do one together: \"A small fish swims into view.\" That happens out in the water, before the heron does anything. So it's the stimulus! You sort the rest.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The hidden step]\n\nThe pink box is the brain. No card goes there, because no camera can see inside the heron's head. Remember that for your CER.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.\n\n## Self-Check · how it works · g4sci-12-1-quiz\n\n[ON SCREEN: Self-Check]\n\nThis Self-Check isn't graded. It's just for you. Answer one question, then press Next question. Picked a wrong answer? Read the hint. It tells you what to think about. Think about the four steps, and try again.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case + roadmap · g4sci-12-1-assignment\n\nYou made it to the assignment!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nFirst, crack the case. Still locked? Tap \"Show me where the clues are,\" then \"Go there.\" With all four clues, pick the answer that fits every clue. Did all four animals react to the same thing?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Today’s parts]\n\nThen there are three parts. Part B: two answers in complete sentences. Part C: make up your own Florida animal moment. A sketch is a bonus. And last, a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Saved for you]\n\nEverything saves itself and shows up in Gathered Answers at the bottom. That's what you turn in.\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants a real field report.\n\n## Part B · short answers · g4sci-12-1-partb\n\n[ON SCREEN: Part B tools]\n\nAnswer three questions in complete sentences, using our step words. Under each box, the \"Your answer needs\" chips turn green as you include each part. Stuck? Press \"Need a hint?\" for one hint at a time. And \"My evidence\" adds anything you highlighted.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach · CAM 2 woods]\n\nFor question five, pick an animal from last night's cameras. They're right above the question.\n\n## Part C · your own animal + sketch · g4sci-12-1-partc\n\nNow you're the scientist.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your scenario]\n\nMake up a short moment, like the heron story, about a Florida animal that wasn't in today's lesson. A pelican? A bobcat? A dolphin? Fill in the four boxes: what happened, what noticed it, what the brain decided, and what the animal did.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The sketch pad]\n\nIf you have time, sketch it too. It's a bonus. Draw with the pen. To add a label, pick Label, drag from a part of your drawing to an empty spot, and type. Add arrows to show the order.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-12-1-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: The classmate says]\n\nA classmate says, \"Animals just react. They don't really need a brain. Their senses do all the work.\" Hmm. Think about the hidden pink box from the heron. Do you agree?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER: your claim, your evidence from today's lesson, and your reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap \"I agree\" or \"I disagree,\" then tap two pieces of evidence. They drop right into your boxes! The reasoning is all you. Try starting with \"This shows that…\"\n\n## Case closed · finish strong · g4sci-12-1-closing\n\n[ON SCREEN: Case closed · Confetti]\n\nCase closed! You did it, scientists.\n\nFour animals noticed four different things with four different senses, and each one's brain decided what to do. The four steps solved it.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Before you go]\n\nTwo last things. Check every box in What to Turn In to earn a heron feather. And download your field journal.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes has a sneak peek at next time.\n"},"pics":{"oak":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","owl":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","fallen":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","fungus":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","liveoak":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","cam1":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","cam2":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","cam3":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","cam4":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","heron":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","manatee":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","pathway":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"},"picCaps":{"oak":"BEFORE · the big live oak · June","owl":"BEFORE · barred owl up in the limbs · June","fallen":"AFTER · the oak knocked over · August","fungus":"AFTER · mushrooms on the trunk · October","liveoak":"Southern live oak with resurrection ferns (CC0)","cam1":"CAM 1 · beach · 9:14:08 PM · sea turtle hatchling","cam2":"CAM 2 · woods · 9:14:31 PM · Florida panther","cam3":"CAM 3 · river · 9:15:02 PM · Florida manatee","cam4":"CAM 4 · marsh · 9:15:47 PM · cottonmouth","heron":"Great blue heron, Everglades National Park (NPS)","manatee":"Florida manatee, Crystal River (USGS)","pathway":"An alligator hears a splash: the four-step pathway"}};
